        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 776,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Repeats an array end to end - and, with more than one count, in rows and columns as well, like tiles on a floor.\n\nnp.tile 3 of 1 2 3 gives 1 2 3 1 2 3 1 2 3: the whole array three times over.\nnp.repeat is different: it repeats each ELEMENT in place (1 1 1 2 2 2 3 3 3).\n\nUse it to loop a pattern to fill a length, to copy one row into every row of a matrix (one offset for every frame, one colour for every point), or to build a grid from a single row.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.tile <counts: ints ...>\nDefault: 2.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nnp.tile 4\nnp.tile 3 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input is repeated and sent.\n\nreps (text box, also an inlet):\nHow many times to repeat along each axis, the last count for the last axis. 3 repeats along the row; 2 1 makes 2 rows of the original row; 2 2 a 2 x 2 block of copies.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntiled:\nThe repeated array.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 2 3 tiled 3 times along its length, and 2 1 times: two rows, each a single copy. A metro re-sends the array five times a second; edit the counts in reps.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.repeat repeats each element in place. np.stack and np.concatenate join different arrays. t.tile is the PyTorch version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Repeats an array end to end - and, with more than one count, in rows and columns as well, like tiles on a floor.\n\nnp.tile 3 of 1 2 3 gives 1 2 3 1 2 3 1 2 3: the whole array three times over.\nnp.repeat is different: it repeats each ELEMENT in place (1 1 1 2 2 2 3 3 3).\n\nUse it to loop a pattern to fill a length, to copy one row into every row of a matrix (one offset for every frame, one colour for every point), or to build a grid from a single row.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.tile <counts: ints ...>\nDefault: 2.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nnp.tile 4\nnp.tile 3 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input is repeated and sent.\n\nreps (text box, also an inlet):\nHow many times to repeat along each axis, the last count for the last axis. 3 repeats along the row; 2 1 makes 2 rows of the original row; 2 2 a 2 x 2 block of copies.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntiled:\nThe repeated array.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 2 3 tiled 3 times along its length, and 2 1 times: two rows, each a single copy. A metro re-sends the array five times a second; edit the counts in reps.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.repeat repeats each element in place. np.stack and np.concatenate join different arrays. t.tile is the PyTorch version."
        },
